        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The pulse oximeter is a medical device used to continuously monitor the percentage of hemoglobin oxygen saturation in the blood.  A noninvasive probe, attached to the skin of the earlobe or finger, uses a red/infrared light sensor to measure the absorptive properties of hemoglobin.  The oximeter can continuously measure functional oxygen saturation in non-encumbered subjects based on these readings for up to 24 hours.   The unit digitally displays, records, and stores the percent O<sub>2</sub> saturation and pulse rate and the pulse signal amplitude.  A battery backup provides for 24 hours of function.  The pulse oximeter has the capability to:<P>
<LI>Monitor blood oxygen saturation ranges of 70-100%.</LI><LI>Maintain 2% accuracy for readings between 70 and 89.9% O<sub>2</sub> saturation and a 1.5% accuracy between 90 and 100% O<sub>2</sub> saturation.</LI><LI>Maintain a pulse rate accuracy of +/- 1 BPM for readings between 20 - 250 BPM.</LI><LI>Provide sensor wavelengths of 660 nm for red, and 920 nm for infrared.</LI><LI>Provide selectable readout response: normal (5-7 seconds) fast (2-3 seconds) or slow (10-15 seconds).</LI><P>The pulse oximeter has the following dimensions:  Height (cm): 5.08, Width (cm): 7.62, Length (cm): 13.97, Mass (kg): 7.28.<end>
        </div>

